<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Scott returns two weeks after his pre-expedition assessment, on the day of his consultation with Dr. Nwankwo-Lund. He took two home readings morning and evening for seven days with a validated upper-arm monitor; the average of the 28 readings is 138/88 mmHg, with no single reading above 150/95. He has no headache, chest pain, breathlessness, or visual change. He stopped his pipe on his quit date a week ago and takes varenicline, with mild nausea after the morning dose. He has started to use less salt. He asked whether a diagnosis of high blood pressure would count against him in leading the sledge journeys; he was told it is common, treatable, and not in itself a reason to stop him. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Home blood pressure</td><td>Seven-day average 138/88 mmHg (28 readings)</td></tr><tr><td>Symptoms</td><td>None</td></tr><tr><td>Tobacco</td><td>No pipe for a week; varenicline with mild nausea</td></tr><tr><td>Concern</td><td>Whether the diagnosis limits his role on the expedition</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

