    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Essential hypertension, stage 1 (I10)</content>. The elevated office reading at intake is now confirmed by home readings: a seven-day average of 138/88 mmHg is stage 1 hypertension. Intake tests show no target-organ damage or secondary cause: sinus rhythm without left ventricular hypertrophy on the ECG, creatinine 0.92 mg/dL (eGFR 107), potassium 4.2 mmol/L, sodium 140 mmol/L, fasting glucose 92 mg/dL, HbA1c 5.3%, TSH 2.1 mIU/L, and LDL cholesterol 122 mg/dL. No urine sample was given at intake. His ten-year cardiovascular risk is low: about 5% by the Pooled Cohort Equations counting him as a smoker, and below the 7.5% threshold by the PREVENT equations, so the guideline advises lifestyle measures for three to six months before medicine. Dr. Nwankwo-Lund agrees, and advises an angiotensin receptor blocker, rather than a thiazide or a beta-blocker, if medicine is needed, started at least four weeks before departure. [S]<list><item>Lifestyle: sodium below 2,300 mg a day, aiming lower, more fruit and vegetables, endurance training at least 150 minutes a week, alcohol within limits, and no tobacco.</item><item>Home readings for a week before each visit.</item><item>Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio before the next visit.</item><item>Review in four weeks; decide about medicine at about three months.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Nicotine dependence, pipe tobacco (F17.290)</content>. No tobacco for a week since his quit date; nausea on varenicline is mild. [S]<list><item>Take varenicline after food with a full glass of water.</item><item>Counseling call each week.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Office blood pressure, validated technique, average of two readings (target below 130/80 mmHg)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>140/88 mmHg</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Home blood pressure monitoring to confirm the diagnosis and guide treatment</td><td>At diagnosis and when treatment changes</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seven-day average 138/88 mmHg; confirms stage 1 hypertension</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Weight, BMI, and cardiac auscultation</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>72.2 kg, BMI 24.1; normal heart sounds</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Basic metabolic panel: sodium, potassium, creatinine, eGFR</td><td>Baseline; 2 to 4 weeks after starting an ARB; then every 6 months on an ARB</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Baseline at intake: sodium 140, potassium 4.2 mmol/L, creatinine 0.92 mg/dL, eGFR 107; not on an ARB</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio</td><td>At diagnosis, then yearly</td><td>Deferred</td><td>Not done: no urine sample was given at intake, and the gap was noticed today. Ordered, to be collected before the next visit</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Lipid panel and HbA1c or fasting glucose</td><td>At diagnosis, then yearly</td><td>Up to date</td><td>At intake: LDL cholesterol 122 mg/dL, HbA1c 5.3%, fasting glucose 92 mg/dL</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>TSH</td><td>At diagnosis, then as indicated</td><td>Up to date</td><td>2.1 mIU/L at intake</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Twelve-lead ECG</td><td>At diagnosis, then as indicated</td><td>Up to date</td><td>At intake: sinus rhythm; no left ventricular hypertrophy</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Echocardiogram</td><td>Only for stage 2 hypertension, suspected left ventricular hypertrophy, or clinical change</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Stage 1 hypertension with no sign of left ventricular hypertrophy</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Fundoscopic examination</td><td>Yearly</td><td>Due</td><td>At the preventive visit</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Ten-year cardiovascular risk estimate</td><td>At diagnosis, then yearly</td><td>Completed today</td><td>About 5% by the Pooled Cohort Equations as a smoker; below 7.5% by PREVENT; lifestyle first</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>PHQ-9 depression screen</td><td>Yearly with hypertension, and before and after a long isolated posting</td><td>Up to date</td><td>PHQ-9 3 at intake</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>GAD-7 anxiety screen</td><td>Before and after a long isolated posting</td><td>Up to date</td><td>GAD-7 4 at intake</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Tobacco use and cessation support (varenicline with counseling)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Abstinent since the quit date a week ago; mild nausea on varenicline</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Wilderness and expedition medicine consultation</td><td>Once, before a long polar expedition</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seen by Dr. Nwankwo-Lund today</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Expedition nutrition plan: energy, weight, and vitamin C</td><td>Planned before departure, then weekly in the field</td><td>Ordered</td><td>Plan made with the consultant today</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Frostbite and hypothermia prevention and field treatment training</td><td>Before departure</td><td>Ordered</td><td>Wilderness first aid course with cold-injury modules booked</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Home blood pressure average below 130/80 mmHg before departure. In progress; target 1910-05-26 [S]</item><item>No tobacco from the quit date, confirmed at each visit. In progress; target 1910-05-26 [S]</item><item>Complete a wilderness first aid course with frostbite, hypothermia and evacuation practice before departure. In progress; target 1910-04-21 [S]</item><item>A written expedition medical plan covering nutrition and vitamin C, cold injury, altitude, mental health support, medicines and evacuation, agreed before departure. In progress; target 1910-05-26 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: 2025 AHA/ACC/AANP/AAPA/ABC/ACCP/ACPM/AGS/AMA/ASPC/NMA/PCNA/SGIM Guideline for the Prevention, Detection, Evaluation and Management of High Blood Pressure in Adults (Jones DW, et al. Circulation 2025;152(11):e114-e218) (2025); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, hypertension monograph (2026); Wilderness Medical Society Clinical Practice Guidelines for the Prevention and Treatment of Frostbite: 2024 Update (McIntosh SE, et al. Wilderness Environ Med 2024;35(2):183-197) (2024); Wilderness Medical Society Clinical Practice Guidelines for the Out-of-Hospital Evaluation and Treatment of Accidental Hypothermia: 2019 Update (Dow J, et al. Wilderness Environ Med 2019;30(4S):S47-S69) (2019); Tobacco Smoking Cessation in Adults, Including Pregnant Persons: Interventions (2021)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Review in four weeks with home readings and the urine test. Next scheduled visit 1910-02-24: six-week review: blood pressure on lifestyle measures, tobacco, urine albumin, dental and hearing results. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
